1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,286
Coach: Several useful contributions do not yet make a shared proposal.

2
00:00:04,286 --> 00:00:09,429
Coach: First find out what each person has offered and what remains unsettled.

3
00:00:09,429 --> 00:00:13,286
Coach: A good summary makes the discussion easier to inspect.

4
00:00:13,286 --> 00:00:19,286
Coach: It does not turn the person summarising into the owner of everyone else's ideas.

5
00:00:19,636 --> 00:00:24,350
Coach: Our practice group is planning a thirty-minute book-club taster.

6
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:28,207
Coach: It should let newcomers try discussing a short text

7
00:00:28,207 --> 00:00:31,208
Coach: and learn how the weekly club works.

8
00:00:31,208 --> 00:00:35,065
Coach: The main room and the stated materials are available.

9
00:00:35,065 --> 00:00:39,779
Coach: At least five minutes for questions belongs inside the thirty minutes.

10
00:00:40,129 --> 00:00:43,986
Coach: Now look at three proposals already on the table.

11
00:00:43,986 --> 00:00:48,272
Coach: Keep each person's words available while you make shorter notes.

12
00:00:48,272 --> 00:00:52,129
Coach: A label such as pairs is useful for navigation,

13
00:00:52,129 --> 00:00:58,986
Coach: but it should not replace the important detail about discussing one line before whole-group sharing.

14
00:00:59,336 --> 00:01:06,622
Maya: Pairs could compare one line from the short text before we share anything with the whole group.

15
00:01:06,972 --> 00:01:10,401
Coach: That is a proposal about participation and sequence.

16
00:01:10,401 --> 00:01:14,258
Coach: We can ask why starting in pairs might help,

17
00:01:14,258 --> 00:01:19,829
Coach: but we should not silently add a reason that Maya has not given.

18
00:01:19,829 --> 00:01:25,401
Coach: Keep an offered action distinct from your own interpretation of its possible benefit.

19
00:01:25,751 --> 00:01:31,322
Noah: A short demonstration could show newcomers what it means to give an interpretation

20
00:01:31,322 --> 00:01:32,608
Noah: and a reason.

21
00:01:32,958 --> 00:01:37,672
Coach: Noah offers a short example and says what it would show.

22
00:01:37,672 --> 00:01:41,101
Coach: That purpose may connect with the pair task:

23
00:01:41,101 --> 00:01:45,815
Coach: newcomers could see the kind of contribution they will then try.

24
00:01:45,815 --> 00:01:47,958
Coach: It is a possible connection,

25
00:01:47,958 --> 00:01:52,244
Coach: not evidence that every newcomer will understand or feel ready.

26
00:01:52,594 --> 00:01:56,451
You: I suggest keeping time for questions about the club,

27
00:01:56,451 --> 00:02:00,308
You: using the information sheet for anything it actually answers.

28
00:02:08,658 --> 00:02:12,944
Coach: Question time is not merely a third person's favourite idea.

29
00:02:12,944 --> 00:02:16,801
Coach: The prompt requires at least five minutes for it.

30
00:02:16,801 --> 00:02:20,658
Coach: By contrast, the order and length of the example,

31
00:02:20,658 --> 00:02:23,658
Coach: pair discussion and sharing are still proposals.

32
00:02:23,658 --> 00:02:25,801
Coach: Put these on different rows

33
00:02:25,801 --> 00:02:29,658
Coach: so a suggestion does not quietly override a requirement.

34
00:02:30,008 --> 00:02:34,294
Coach: The available information sheet can support answers about the club.

35
00:02:34,294 --> 00:02:37,294
Coach: Its full contents are not supplied here,

36
00:02:37,294 --> 00:02:41,151
Coach: so we cannot promise it answers every visitor's question.

37
00:02:41,151 --> 00:02:44,151
Coach: A useful note can preserve that limit:

38
00:02:44,151 --> 00:02:49,294
Coach: answer what the sheet covers, and identify anything that still needs checking.

39
00:02:49,644 --> 00:02:54,787
Coach: It is tempting to tidy the board by removing repetition or uncertainty.

40
00:02:54,787 --> 00:02:57,358
Coach: Remove duplicate wording if it helps,

41
00:02:57,358 --> 00:03:00,358
Coach: but keep different conditions and unresolved questions.

42
00:03:00,358 --> 00:03:04,644
Coach: Two people can favour the same element for different reasons,

43
00:03:04,644 --> 00:03:09,787
Coach: and those reasons may matter when you decide how to use it.

44
00:03:22,137 --> 00:03:26,851
Coach: Before combining anything, check your map against what was actually said.

45
00:03:26,851 --> 00:03:31,566
Coach: Have you kept the main proposal, the reason that was supplied,

46
00:03:31,566 --> 00:03:34,566
Coach: the fixed limits and the unresolved question?

47
00:03:34,566 --> 00:03:38,851
Coach: You do not need four boxes in every real conversation;

48
00:03:38,851 --> 00:03:43,137
Coach: you need enough structure to avoid losing a material distinction.

49
00:03:43,487 --> 00:03:47,773
Coach: The next step is not to announce that everyone agrees.

50
00:03:47,773 --> 00:03:52,058
Coach: It is to ask which parts already have explicit support

51
00:03:52,058 --> 00:03:54,201
Coach: and which still need discussion.

52
00:03:54,201 --> 00:04:01,487
Coach: A clear map gives the group something concrete to correct before you invest in a combined plan.
